A||||15||87GTZ34||Turbo owners what mods ....||||5||
Z||000000||87GTZ34||10-22-2000||05:29 PM||||Gals & guys running turbos...  <BR>what computer/prom mods have you made;<BR>what exhaust/muffler/cat are you running;<BR>who's system and how much boost?<P>Inquiring minds want to know.  I am scoping out a turbo system for my '96 setup. I bought the Borla exhaust before getting some backpressure figures.  Any & all info is appreciated before I start making more ignorant purchases.<P>Thanks for helping!<BR><P>------------------<BR>87GTZ34||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||10-22-2000||08:40 PM||||Ive got a turbo system on my 3.1. its supposed to be an AirResearch kit, I bought entire kit, uninstalled from the original buyer, dont think its made anymore. I did put a performance chip in and upgraded to accell coil. everything to install came in the box, turbo, tubes, vacume line, wastegate, exhaust from manifolds back. It does include a cat converter, says the back pressure is needed for best performance. Ive experimented with diff pressure and found that about 5 pnds boost is plenty for my car. Feels like I added 150 hp. I dont have ANY lag, power comes on even rite off idle all the way to redline. it also had in the kit one of the split type mufflers running from center inlet and out both ends to dual tips. Im very happy with it, glad I did it instead of the V8 swap. I eat stock Mustang GTs and IROCs - with engines twice as big. You could probably even improve your turbo performance with injector upgrades and larger throttle body too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||10-24-2000||11:08 PM||||Roger thanks for sharing the info.  Do you know who makes the cat & muffler? <BR>WOW! Only one turboed Fiero in the universe and 3.1 at that.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I'm shocked and amazed, I would have thought there would be at least a dozen or so with all the tail kickin stories floating around the forum.<P>------------------<BR>87GTZ34<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||11
Z||000003||Predator||10-25-2000||07:28 AM||||I have the Design1Systems Stage II kit. I am currently installing it so I can't tell you any performance numbers. I am working with the owner Kevin Leslie with the installation since the kit I bought from a person was made for a 1988 and my Fiero is a 1985 GT. There are a few differences in the parts. Kevin is extremely helpful and willing to work with you on a conversion. He is going to swap a 1985 exhaust for mine and all I have to pay is the shipping. I am also sending him pictures of the parts so he can match up what I should have to what I have so I can make sure the kit is complete when I am finished. He estimates the HP to be around 225 after the kit is installed, and that is plenty of power for me in this little two seater   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I want my car to go, but not out from under me. I will post a update when I am done and can actually start driving my car again. I have it partially dissasembled for the kit. Man I am already getting the shakes not being able to drive it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Predator (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000004||Hartz||10-25-2000||12:51 PM||||OK, OK, I admit - I have a turbo, too!  <P>The kit was originally purchased from Fiero Conversions, but we junked about half of it.  I did keep the turbo, the PROM, and some of the fittings.  Here's the list of stuff done to the engine:<P>Parts:<P>ARP Head and Main studs, washers, nuts, rod bolts, and nuts.<BR>Accel 8.8 Spiral Core plug wires.<BR>MSD 6BTM Ignition (Boost Timing Master version of 6AL)<BR>Accel Super Coil<BR>JE Pistons, custom forged aluminum (8.57:1 CR), tool steel pins, locks, and rings.<BR>Crane Compucam and Lifters (P/N 254122)<BR>Crane Valve Springs (P/N 99848-12)<BR>Crane Retainers (P/N 99915-12)<BR>Crane Locks (P/N 99041-1)<BR>Valve Seals<BR>3.1 Liter Crankshaft (General Motors)<BR>Melling High Volume oil pump<BR>Air Research T-10 Turbocharger<BR>Fel Pro Gasket set with O-Ring Head Gasket.<BR>Rod, Main, and Cam Bearings<BR>AC  R41-TS Spark Plugs<BR>Cloyes True Roller Timing set<BR>TH125-C Automatic Transmission shift kit<BR>Custom 2 1/2" mandral-bent exhaust system<BR>Supertrapp Stainless Steel 4A Muffer system (P/N 446-2010)<P>Machine Work:<P>Balance and Blueprint<BR>Crossdrill crank, straighten and polish.<BR>Valve job - full radius, hand lapped and blended<BR>Resurface Heads.<BR>Cut Decks<BR>Float Rods<BR>Bore and Hone with Deck Plate<BR>Port Cylinder Heads <BR>Port Match exhaust manifolds<BR>Port Match Intake Manifold and Runners<BR>Polish side beams on rods<BR>Fabricate custom air intake to replace stock air inlet.<P>It runs pretty well.  I've still got a little bit of tuning to do, but it'll snap you back in your seat.  Right now she only runs maybe 5 lbs. of boost, and I'd like to twist that up a notch eventually, but I've been pretty happy with it.  Was it the cheapest or easiest way to make power?  Maybe not.  I think if I had it to do over again, I'd think long and hard about a S/C 3800.  But it's fun and it's different, and I like it.<P>Take care,<BR>Hartz<BR>'85 Fiero SE<P>||||reg||7
Z||000005||mrfixit58||10-25-2000||01:29 PM||||I wrote the following on another thread but thought it would be better here. Better, if for no other reason than future archive searches.<P>***********************************<P>According to the turbocharging book I just read, building a motor specifically for turbocharging is not necessary unless you plan on running the boost past 11:1 (or so). hugh was right on with his discription but it may be overkill if you plan on keeping boost below 9 psi.<BR>Like terryk mentioned, if you are running a higher compression motor, you will need a spark management system to keep your motor from detonation. I have been looking at vaious systems that automatically adjust the timing based on a knock sensor. This type should provided maximum timing (a.k.a. max power) without damaging your motor. And, as an added benifit, if you increase the octane rating of your fuel, it will automatically adjust the timing as well.<P>To beef up the motor to handle more boost, you may consider early 327 SBC rods that have been resized to fit. You can still buy peened (sp?) rods for this motor and their strength should be sufficient. Forged pistons are best but can be VERY expensive and difficult to find for 2.8 and 3.4s. Hyper tech pistion are better than cast and will buy you a couple more pounds of boost.<P>Stock exhaust manifolds will work great. Using headers may slow the exhaust gases which will slow the spool time of your turbo (a.k.a turbo lag). A free flowing exhaust after the turbo is a good thing if your going to be running high boost numbers.<P>If your serious about making a turbo system for your car, I encourage you to buy a book on the subject and do a little homework. If you like, I'll dig up my turbo book and tell you the author and title. Or, do like I did and check with yor local speed shop. They usually carry books that tell you how to build almost anything... including turbocharging. Expect to pay about $20... but money well spent.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||10-25-2000||04:26 PM||||Does anyone knows if IRM still selling their system? I may add a turbo to my 3.4 but would like to keep the Borla exaust that I just bought. I remember that the IRM keeps the stock exaust. Still will have to give away my headers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> and put back stock mflds.<BR>Hey Ron, did you finished that DOHC?||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||10-27-2000||01:34 AM||||Thanks for the input.  I've been studying up on the turbo stuff and have been trading emails 3 turbo shops.  They all tell me to leave the OBD-II alone.  BTW it has two knock sensors... I think someone on eng team was anticipating pressure.<BR>Roy & Hartz, one other trick to keeping the bottom end together under 'big' pressure is to plate it.  A metal plate bolts to the engine bottom and cap bolts.  This of course is expensive and has to be fabricated by a professional shop.<P>------------------<BR>87GTZ34||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rednax Asbestos||10-27-2000||04:15 AM||||Hi people,<P>This is my first post as you can see but i just had to skip in on this subject. I own a '86 GT fastback and I am located in Holland (europe). I have been telling everyone that i'm going to turbo charge the engine and now I'll have to prove it. I was thinking of using a aftermarket programable ECM (haltech) and using a garret T3 turbo charger of a 2.2 liter engine. I will also use a 2 bar MAP sensor. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||turbo86se||10-27-2000||10:53 AM||||I've got IRM's older MillerWoods turbo system on my 86SE. Bought and installed at IRM. <BR>Manifolds are ported and polished,  coated inside and out by Jet Hot.<BR>Removed cat. convertor, stock muffler.<BR>Running 8-9lbs at full-boost.<BR>I'm using the computer/PROM that came with the system. <BR>53K fun-filled miles, still runs perfect.<P>Only raced one car, an early 90's Vette.<BR>He got a car-length head start of me, and I shot past him like he was standing still!!  You should have seen his face!<P>Oh BTW, if you're going to be in Jacksonville tomorrow for Robert's meeting and cruise, I'll be there!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000010||87GTZ34||10-27-2000||02:01 PM||||Alex,  Didn't see your post.  I think I will try to talk with Borla directly on the use of their mufflers with Turbos.  Also, unless some little elves ventured over from the cookie factory into my garage,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I think it is still sitting there as I have been travelling steadily since July.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>87GTZ34||||reg||1
Z||000011||Hartz||10-27-2000||02:56 PM||||OBD-II didn't start until '95 or '96, which obviously means we don't have to worry about it on our Fieros.  Me thinks whomever you were talking to about this was thinking "Firebird", not Fiero.  I know the Firebirds have extensive knock retard systems.  I have never heard of such a thing on Fieros.<P>I think your Borla would be just fine with a turbo.  Don't sweat it.  That would be the least of your worries on a system like this.<P>Enjoy,<BR>Hartz||||reg||4
Z||000012||Alex4mula||10-27-2000||03:12 PM||||87GTZ34 is planning his turbo for a DOHC 3.4 conversion he is working on. I think his donor car engine computer is OBC-II. That's why he's checking that.||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTZ34||10-28-2000||09:42 AM||||Alex is correct.  I have a 3.4 DOHC/4T60E setup from a '96 Monte Carlo Z34.  I am retaining the '96 PCM controls as I thought a flash programmer would have been out for the MC/GP by now.  I was wrong...  Anyway the turbo folks have all stayed consistent and said don't change maps just convert to plus atm sensor on the '96. Of course injectors may need to be 'upgraded'.<P>------------------<BR>87GTZ34||||reg||1
